---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Newsletter from '../components/Newsletter.astro';

const themes = [
  {
    title: 'Clarity',
    body: 'Seeing things as they are—not as we were taught, promised, or pressured to see them—is the beginning of every good decision.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Reinvention',
    body: 'Starting over is not failure. Sometimes it is the first honest decision a woman gets to make for herself.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Financial Awareness',
    body: 'Money is not a dirty word. Silence around it can be devastating. Women deserve full, honest knowledge of their financial lives.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Friendship',
    body: 'Trusted friendships do more than comfort us. The people who know us best can help us see the blind spots we cannot see for ourselves.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Courage',
    body: 'Not the loud kind, but the quiet, daily courage to name what is true, leave what no longer fits, and begin again.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Moral Ambiguity',
    body: 'There is rarely one clean, correct way to navigate a difficult situation. Understanding a choice is not the same as excusing it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Institutional Accountability',
    body: 'Institutions have responsibilities beyond protecting themselves. Passing a problem along does not resolve it.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  active="home"
  bodyClass="home-dark"
  title="Monica Messer — Author of The Family Narrative"
  description="Monica Messer writes character-driven fiction about women, families, and institutions. Her debut novel, The Family Narrative, is complete at approximately 72,000 words."
>
  <!-- HERO — dark/reverse -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Monica Messer</p>
        <h1>Stories about marriage, motherhood, money, and the family dynamics, institutions and legacies that shape us.</h1>
        <div class="btn-group">
          <a href="/the-family-narrative/" class="btn btn-primary">The Family Narrative</a>
          <a href="/read/" class="btn btn-outline">Read Chapter One</a>
          <a href="/read/#listen" class="btn btn-outline">Listen to Chapter One</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-media">
        <img src="/assets/author-portrait.jpg" alt="Portrait of Monica Messer" class="cover-img hero-portrait" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FEATURED BOOK — light -->
  <section class="bg-sand">
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="split-media">
        <img src="/assets/family-narrative-3d.png" alt="The Family Narrative by Monica Messer" class="cover-img" />
      </div>
      <div class="split-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">The Family Narrative</p>
        <h2>Every family has a story.</h2>
        <p>
          Frances Stanton has spent decades building a life that works. A respected professor. A beautiful
          home in Los Gatos. Two sons nearly grown. A husband whose family name opens doors at the
          university, in the community, and in every room that matters.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then, over lunch on her fifty-fourth birthday, Frances overhears a conversation that makes
          her question everything she thought she knew about her marriage.
        </p>
        <p>
          At first, she believes the betrayal is an affair. But as the truth surfaces, Frances discovers
          something far larger: hidden debt, addiction, family complicity, institutional silence, and a
          carefully protected version of the Stanton story that everyone around her seemed to understand
          before she did.
        </p>
        <p>
          When her husband files for divorce before she has even caught up to the truth, Frances is forced
          to confront not only what he has hidden, but what she allowed herself not to examine—especially
          the financial life she trusted him to manage.
        </p>
        <p>
          To move forward, she must learn how to protect herself without losing her compassion, how to
          tell her sons the truth without asking them to inherit its burden, and how to decide what parts
          of the Stanton story are worth carrying forward.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>The Family Narrative</em> is a novel about marriage, money, reputation, and the stories
          families preserve—until someone decides the truth matters more.
        </p>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1.5em;">
          What do we owe the stories we inherit—and what do we choose to pass on?
        </p>
        <div class="btn-group">
          <a href="/the-family-narrative/" class="btn btn-primary">The Family Narrative</a>
          <a href="/read/" class="btn btn-outline">Read Chapter One</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container narrow" style="margin-top:88px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Book Clubs</p>
      <h2>Is <em>The Family Narrative</em> Right for Your Book Club?</h2>
      <p>
        <em>The Family Narrative</em> is built for the kind of conversation that continues after everyone
        closes the book.
      </p>
      <p>
        At its center is a marriage coming apart, but its questions reach far beyond whether Frances should
        stay or leave. What do partners owe one another about the past? When does trust become
        disengagement? And how much responsibility do we bear for truths someone else worked to keep from
        us?
      </p>
      <p>
        The novel gives readers characters whose choices can be understood without necessarily being
        defended. Dick's addiction, privilege, and family system raise difficult questions about compassion
        and accountability. His parents force another: when does mercy become enabling, and can the kind of
        second chance that saved one generation damage the next?
      </p>
      <p>
        Frances's choices invite equally rich debate about money, motherhood, marriage, and the truths
        parents owe their children. Ultimately, <em>The Family Narrative</em> asks: What do we want to
        inherit from our families, and what are we willing to leave behind? It offers no easy villains or
        easy absolution—only competing loyalties, morally complicated choices, and plenty of room for
        readers to disagree.
      </p>
      <p class="lede">That is where the conversation begins.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- MONICA'S WRITING THEMES — dark/reverse -->
  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head center">
        <p class="eyebrow">What Monica Writes About</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-cards values-cards">
        {themes.map((t) => (
          <div class="theme-card">
            <h3>{t.title}</h3>
            <p>{t.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHAT I'M WRITING NEXT — light -->
  <section class="bg-sand2">
    <div class="container narrow center">
      <p class="eyebrow">What I'm Writing Next</p>
      <p class="lede measure">
        The questions at the heart of <em>The Family Narrative</em> continue into Monica's next novels:
        What do we owe one another when we recognize something is wrong? When does looking away become
        complicity? And what, intentionally or not, do we pass on to the people who come after us?
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="work-block">
        <div class="split-media">
          <img src="/assets/she-would-be-next-3d.png" alt="She Would Be Next by Monica Messer" class="cover-img" />
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="work-status">A novel in development</p>
          <h2>She Would Be Next</h2>
          <p>
            A medical school removes a troubled physician from its residency program and quietly allows
            him to move on.
          </p>
          <p>Then another institution does the same. And another.</p>
          <p>
            Years later, when members of one of the committees responsible for his dismissal become
            targets, a female physician and professor who served on that committee begins looking back at
            decisions everyone believed were finished.
          </p>
          <p>What she discovers forces her to confront an uncomfortable question:</p>
          <p class="lede">
            When an institution protects itself by passing a problem along, who bears responsibility for
            what happens next?
          </p>
          <p>
            A novel of psychological suspense about professional silence, institutional self-protection,
            and the danger of believing that removing a threat is the same thing as resolving it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="work-block">
        <div class="split-media">
          <img src="/assets/geography-of-everlasting-life-3d.png" alt="The Geography of Everlasting Life by Monica Messer" class="cover-img" />
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="work-status">A novel in development</p>
          <h2>The Geography of Everlasting Life</h2>
          <p>
            After Oliver and Sygne Thorson's lifetime of possessions is divided into numbered lots and sold
            at auction, their belongings scatter throughout a small Nebraska community.
          </p>
          <p>Then something strange begins to happen.</p>
          <p>
            A battered black roasting pan finds its way to a woman who begins cooking for a lonely
            neighbor. A worn pocketknife seems to carry its former owner's instinct to help. A harmonica
            and a porcelain angel begin to matter to their new owners in ways no one can quite explain.
          </p>
          <p>
            Elaine, the auctioneer's mother, knew Oliver and Sygne well enough to recognize what others
            cannot: the buyers have taken home more than objects. Something of the couple—their habits,
            generosity, resilience, wounds, and ways of loving—seems to have traveled with their
            possessions.
          </p>
          <p>
            But as Elaine traces what Oliver and Sygne have left behind, the mystery turns toward her own
            home and the husband she lost years earlier. Her resentment toward the harm he caused has made
            it easier to remember his failures than the man she once loved. Slowly, Elaine begins to wonder
            whether allowing what was good in him to live on requires her to excuse what was not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>The Geography of Everlasting Life</em> is an upmarket novel with elements of magical
            realism about the complicated inheritance of love: what the dead leave behind, what the living
            choose to carry forward, and whether we can forgive what hurt us without forgetting it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE MIDDLE FIVE — light -->
  <section class="bg-sand">
    <div class="container work-block">
      <div class="split-media">
        <img src="/assets/the-middle-five-cover.png" alt="The Middle Five by Francis La Flesche, foreword by Monica Messer" class="cover-img" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="work-status">A public-domain edition</p>
        <h2>The Middle Five</h2>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top:0;">Francis La Flesche &middot; Foreword by Monica Messer</p>
        <p>
          Originally published by Francis La Flesche, <em>The Middle Five</em> offers a rare and deeply
          personal account of Omaha boys navigating life at a nineteenth-century boarding school during a
          period of profound cultural change.
        </p>
        <p>
          Monica discovered La Flesche's work while researching the Omaha Tribe for a novel and was moved
          by the wit, humanity, and historical value of his story. This edition is intended to help bring
          La Flesche's work to modern readers while honoring his voice and perspective.
        </p>
        <p>
          As you read, begin with La Flesche's original preface. It is essential to understanding the
          spirit of the book and the world he was trying to preserve on the page.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-group">
          <a href="/the-middle-five/" class="btn btn-primary">Learn More About The Middle Five</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ABOUT MONICA — dark/reverse -->
  <section>
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="split-media">
        <img src="/assets/author-portrait.jpg" alt="Portrait of Monica Messer" class="cover-img portrait" style="max-width:340px;" />
      </div>
      <div class="split-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">About Monica</p>
        <h2>Monica Messer</h2>
        <p>
          Monica Messer is a debut novelist based in Omaha, Nebraska. After a forty-year career in
          technology and executive leadership, she turned her attention to fiction and to questions that
          have followed her throughout her life: how families construct their stories, how institutions
          protect themselves, how women navigate power and responsibility, and how the choices one
          generation makes shape the next.
        </p>
        <p>
          She spent much of the 1990s in Northern California during the Internet startup era, an experience
          that informs the academic and professional world of <em>The Family Narrative</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          When she isn't in Omaha, Monica spends time in Los Angeles with her daughter and siblings and at
          her home in Belize with her husband, where she particularly loves to write.
        </p>
        <p>
          Her debut novel, <em>The Family Narrative</em>, is complete at approximately 72,000 words.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-group">
          <a href="/about/" class="btn btn-outline">More About Monica</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Newsletter />
</BaseLayout>
